architecture cmd-embed
  canonical_name BonfyreEmbed
  family command
  domain Model / Embedding
  semantic_question What does the BonfyreEmbed capability organ realize, and by what contract?
  purpose Command-surface organ realizing the SID capability. A first-class Bonfyre capability, advertised and composable, not a script.
  maturity implemented
  command BonfyreEmbed
  internal_calculus bonfyre-embed binary
  forbidden_inference the command exists -> its effect is authorized (read-only, but scope still applies)
  invariant a requested backend that could not be honoured is a refusal, not a warning; an ONNX request that silently produced a hashed-token vector exited 0, and hashed-token geometry is not a learned metric so no distance threshold survives the substitution
  invariant the receipt names the backend requested beside the backend obtained, and --allow-degraded is how a caller consents to the difference
  forbidden_inference a zero exit -> the requested representation was produced
  source_path cmd/BonfyreEmbed/src/main.c
  witness cmd/BonfyreEmbed/tests/cache_identity.test.mjs exercises real ONNX cache reuse, changed vocabulary, replaced model bytes and missing model refusal
  invariant single-text ONNX cache identity includes runtime version and hashes of the resolved graph and vocabulary; unreadable assets cannot be satisfied by an old cached vector
  forbidden_inference a model name and vector width -> the same embedding geometry across runtime or asset changes
  known_gap cache identity hashes the selected ONNX graph and vocabulary, not separate ONNX external-data files; externally stored weights need an extended asset manifest before cache equivalence is claimed
  source_path scripts/build_semantic_index.sh
  source_path scripts/semantic_corpus.mjs
  witness scripts/tests/semantic_corpus_test.mjs
  invariant the semantic corpus is a bounded, atomic text projection of retained item rows ordered by observation time; its address keys and 400-character body excerpts preserve the existing embedding input contract
  forbidden_inference a corpus row or computed embedding -> source identity, semantic truth, corroboration, authority, or a valid distance under another model
  witness cmd/BonfyreEmbed/tests/tokenizer_visibility_test.sh
  invariant embed static --store files each visible vector under sha256(schema, .bfse digest, text), so a pack built from it cannot collide with ONNX cache entries for the same text; invisible rows are not stored
  source_lineage the 91-command capability surface
  known_gap responds to --help (live); realizes SID; deeper development toward the BonfyreFS level (WiringSpec consumes/produces, full contract) is ongoing
  known_gap --input-jsonl was in the usage text and was never parsed. The flag was advertised, accepted by the shell and did nothing: the binary fell through to the usage screen, which reads as a syntax mistake by the caller. A corpus arrives as rows, and writing 22,962 titles to individual .txt files to satisfy --input-dir is a detour through the filesystem for no reason.
  known_gap the batch path opened the vector store at the --dims default of 384 while the model produces 768, then inserted without checking the result. It reported two hundred embedded and stored nothing usable; a corpus that reports success and holds unusable rows is worse than one that fails. The store is now opened at the width the first embedding actually returns, and a row of a different width is refused rather than written.
  source_path lib/libbonfyre/include/bf_bert_fold.h
  witness cmd/BonfyreEmbed/tests/tokenizer_visibility_test.sh
  invariant embedding visibility and transactional-store witnesses execute through Bun and bun:sqlite without a Python helper; the contention witness waits for an acknowledged read transaction and fails if its reader exits unsuccessfully
  witness cmd/BonfyreEmbed/Makefile test runs real ONNX visibility, failed-commit accounting and replacement-vector checks when the declared local model is present; an absent model remains an explicit skipped witness rather than trained-model evidence
  invariant a text none of whose content words the vocabulary can build is not embedded. Its vector would encode its word count and nothing else, so every unrelated text of the same length would sit at cosine 1.000 from it; a missing row answers no query, a collapsed row answers every query wrongly
  invariant the pre-tokenizer folds the way uncased BERT folds -- lowercase, NFD, drop nonspacing marks -- from a table generated out of unicodedata, not a hand-kept list of accents
  known_gap the pre-tokenizer walked bytes with ASCII predicates. isspace, ispunct and tolower see nothing above 0x7F, so "women's" with a typographic apostrophe was one word whose tail missed the vocabulary and became [UNK], "São" kept its combining tilde and missed, and uppercase Cyrillic was never lowercased. On this estate's 22,962 headlines that touched 11.8 percent through curly quotes and dashes, 5.4 percent through accented Latin and 4.6 percent through uppercase Cyrillic. "The best rivalry in women's tennis" and "Palmeiras bate o São Paulo" each went from a partially unknown text to zero unknown words.
  known_gap the English vocabulary carries Cyrillic, Bengali, Arabic and CJK as single characters and not as word pieces, so correctly folded text in those scripts is still invisible: "Уроженка Москвы впервые выиграла турнир" is five unknown words of five. That is a property of the model, not the tokenizer, and those titles are now refused rather than stored; searching them needs a multilingual embedder this estate does not have installed.
  known_gap measured on the corpus before the fix: 36 texts sat in cosine-1.000 pairs with unrelated headlines. The first refusal rule caught 21 of them and let 15 through, because it counted a surviving comma as a visible word; the rule now counts content words only.
  known_gap the default model name resolves to a directory that does not exist, so onnx embedding fails over to a refusal unless --model names the installed nomic-embed-text-onnx. The refusal is correct; the default is not.
  known_gap jsonl_reader_open, jsonl_reader_extract, jsonl_reader_close and embed_text_native are defined and called from nowhere -- a JSONL reader was written for the batch mode and never wired, which is how --input-jsonl came to be advertised and unparsed.
  known_gap the vector writer checked no sqlite3_step result and no COMMIT result and returned success unconditionally, and it opened a transaction only when none was active. A single COMMIT failing with SQLITE_BUSY under a concurrent reader left that transaction open; every later insert saw autocommit off, skipped BEGIN and COMMIT, and rode the orphaned transaction until sqlite3_close rolled it back. Resuming the publication corpus it reported 6,963 rows stored and 1,216 landed -- a clean prefix of 2,463 input lines persisted and nothing after -- and the loss was found only because the store's row count failed to match the sum of two runs.
  known_gap texts whose only surviving words were numerals passed the invisibility rule, which counted any visible word. A Japanese truck crash and an Urdu shooting that each kept a "4" embedded at cosine 0.980, the highest cross-feed pair in the corpus after the fully invisible texts were removed.
  known_gap resident memory held at 185-204 MB across a 46-sample resumed batch, so the earlier kill of the full embedding run was system memory pressure and not growth in the batch loop.
  witness cmd/BonfyreEmbed/tests/store_contention_test.sh
  witness cmd/BonfyreEmbed/tests/agreement_test.sh checks agree against the real teacher and .bfse: refusals before any load, invisible texts counted not compared, exact sha256 of graph, .bfse and corpus in the receipt, and exactly one memory grant of 2 x graph bytes taken and released
  witness estate/generated/nomic-static-128-teacher-agreement-site-passages-2026-10-30.json: nomic-static-128 shares 18.5 percent of top-10 neighbours with its teacher over 1703 site passages; peak footprint 279,939,456 bytes against a 274,592,584-byte grant
  invariant bonfyre-embed agree asks the ResourceAdmission memory ledger before loading the ONNX teacher, refuses (exit 3) without a grant, releases the grant when the session closes, and reports the measured peak footprint beside the estimate so the 2 x graph rule can be checked
  forbidden_inference neighbour overlap with the teacher -> retrieval quality, agreement on another corpus, or proof the .bfse was distilled from that teacher
  known_gap the agree estimate rule (2 x graph bytes) ran about 2 percent under the measured peak on the 1703-passage run; the grant is an estimate, not a ceiling, and the single-text and batch embed paths still load ONNX without a memory grant
  invariant a batch reports the rows the store holds, not the rows it tried to write. Every step and every COMMIT is checked, the writer writes only inside a transaction it opened, a failed commit is rolled back and counted not_stored, and a batch with any row not stored exits nonzero. Under a reader holding the store for eight seconds the witness saw 47 reported, 47 landed, 1 not stored, exit 1 -- where the previous writer would have reported all 48 and rolled most of them back on close
  invariant a text whose only visible words are numerals is refused as invisible; a surviving digit does not identify a text
  internal_calculus prune: --prune-invisible with --input-jsonl and --insert-db deletes stored rows the current visibility rule would refuse, by tokenizing alone and never running the model. On the publication store it examined 22,962 titles, found 1,542 invisible, and removed the 294 numeral-only rows the two earlier runs had stored
  known_gap the single-text path also called the writer without checking it and printed "Inserted ... into ..." after a failed commit; it now reports the failure and exits nonzero
  invariant a text is refused when its unknown words outnumber its meaningful visible words. This follows from mean pooling rather than from a tuned threshold: every unknown word contributes the same [UNK] token, so once they are the majority the shared [UNK] mass outweighs what the visible words say. On a copy of the store it removed 159 rows beyond the numeral rule
  invariant re-embedding an id replaces its vector. sqlite-vec's vec0 does not honour OR REPLACE on its primary key -- a second insert fails with a UNIQUE constraint and the first vector stays -- so the writer deletes the id's vector and inserts inside one transaction
  known_gap the collapse the invisibility rule was written to stop survived it in a subtler form: texts with seven of eight words unknown kept one incidental token and collapsed onto it. Two differently worded Russian headlines about a Zelensky-Putin meeting sat at cosine 1.000 on "g20" and two Ukrainian drone headlines at 1.000 on "sargan". Because the surviving token was the topic, the pairs read as cross-feed corroboration and headed the corroboration list, which is the most dangerous way a representation can fail.
  known_gap under the unchecked writer, re-embedding a changed text replaced its artifacts row and, because vec0 refused the replacement silently, kept the old vector beside the new text. The store would have described one headline and returned another headline's neighbours. Found by testing replacement on a scratch store before relying on it, not by reading the insert.

