  forbidden_inference a smaller pack and lower memory -> a faster model; on the same consumer the v9 pack prefilled 8.7 times slower and decoded 11.8 times slower than the FP16 passthrough
  forbidden_inference 40 of 48 tokens agreeing on one prompt -> preserved generation quality; no task score, perplexity or second prompt was measured, and agreement is with the passthrough pack, not with the original framework
  forbidden_inference the FP16 passthrough receipt -> compressed FPQ execution; passthrough carries no v9 tensor
  source_path cmd/BonfyreQwenFPQ/src/fpq_orc_jit.c
  source_path cmd/BonfyreQwenFPQ/src/sli_jit_kernel.c
  source_path cmd/BonfyreQwenFPQ/tests/orc_jit_test.c
  witness cmd/BonfyreQwenFPQ/tests/orc_jit_test.c (make test-orc) runs the SLI kernel on four tensor shapes three ways: its source compiled ordinarily with the shape passed in, the temporary-C path (source written out, a compiler process, a shared object loaded back), and in-process specialisation through LLVM ORC. All three return the same bits for every row, and a shape asked for twice is compiled once
  witness 2026-10-04 LLVM 22.1.7 on this machine: getting specialised code took 10 to 15 ms per shape through ORC after a first shape of about 120 to 165 ms, against 260 to 360 ms through the temporary-C path. Running the kernel cost the same all three ways (for 896 rows by 19 blocks: 23.5 ms unspecialised, 24.4 ms ORC, 23.3 ms temporary-C)
  witness cmd/BonfyreQwenFPQ/tests/vectors/command_behavior.json + packs/no-simulation/command-behavior-20261029.tsv: behavior vectors on small real fixtures in fresh temp dirs, replayed by make -C tests/vectors check (2026-10-29): command.model=pass
  internal_calculus the kernel is compiled to LLVM IR at build time with two sentinel constants where a tensor's rows and blocks-per-row go; at run time the sentinels are replaced, the ordinary O3 pipeline is run for the host's CPU and features, and the code is linked in memory. Strict floating point is kept, so specialising changes no result
  invariant the ORC path writes nothing to /tmp, starts no compiler process, and loads no shared object from a path another user could have written
  forbidden_inference a specialised kernel -> a faster kernel; baking the shape into this kernel bought no measurable run time by either JIT path, so what ORC removes is the cost and the risk of getting the code, not time spent in it
  forbidden_inference the ORC path built and tested -> the ORC path in use; it is behind make ORC=1, the shipped binary enables neither JIT, and no generation has been run through it
  known_gap the temporary-C path it replaces is still in the source for builds that set FPQ_JIT_SLI without ORC; it writes to a predictable /tmp path and loads whatever shared object is there. Neither path is enabled in the default build
  known_gap an ORC build links this machine's LLVM dynamically, so it is a build-node capability; the Makefile refuses to install one. On 2026-10-04 a mistaken make ORC=1 did install one over the managed binary; the previous binary was restored from a copy taken just before, on the assumption that the installed copy and the build-tree copy had been the same file
  known_gap a v9 pack carries no model config of its own: without a .fpq.config.json beside it the runtime guesses the layer count and dies asking for layer 24 of a 24-layer model. The comparison copies the passthrough pack's config
  known_gap v9 generation is one prompt on one small model, at a decode rate too slow to use; the active weight cache, the fused and prefill backends and warm resident mode were not measured against it, and neither was any model larger than 0.5B
  known_gap operator command.model quality-proven on a real input in the command workload of 2026-10-10 (receipt rcpt-feafb0f05916e127396f), one run on one input; realizes QwenFPQCapability; deeper development toward the BonfyreFS level (WiringSpec consumes/produces, full contract) is ongoing

